#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" :question/math-academy-id "e-5502" :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5503" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133" :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee2e3afd-7fde-5290-8795-e227ca576911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306812} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ba1e2f-5e03-522e-b2ca-6479829d6b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 9 \\\\ -21 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306813} {:answer/id #uuid "62899d69-def9-514f-9db2-dea6f520dc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306814} {:answer/id #uuid "79f61a17-ced2-5943-9d14-49c1cc1b5ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 6 \\\\ -18 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306815} {:answer/id #uuid "33b3d3a2-eae4-500e-92a2-bf89207c501f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 9 \\\\ -24 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62899d69-def9-514f-9db2-dea6f520dc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306814} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed03eea-abda-5c25-af7f-1e3dea3a85f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c003b964-3476-5862-b707-26f446b5a877" :question/math-academy-id "q-140132" :question/problem "Find ${C}^{36},$ where\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $C:$\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $C=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${C}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{C}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{36} & =P{D}^{36}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{36}(\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[18]{3})^{36} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[36]{3})^{36}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}27 & -3 \\\\ -27 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e6af79-f7e3-5db5-9c4a-6c5729d1f256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122789} {:answer/id #uuid "beaae8c3-317f-56c8-bf39-e4ee6c67e44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}100 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 100\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122790} {:answer/id #uuid "2719272f-b640-5fe6-bb34-bc2adf1912af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122791} {:answer/id #uuid "70989f08-06bc-54b1-8a9f-32b533a24c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122792} {:answer/id #uuid "75418961-6169-5de1-af74-a3d396e1c377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75418961-6169-5de1-af74-a3d396e1c377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122793} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9de3606-8c8d-5181-a42f-a52d688c7c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6af12003-3389-5965-bc72-a49e9c3974b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-37257" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{101},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{101} & =P{D}^{101}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{101}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{101} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{101}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e42f0896-08db-560d-a6a0-716ad1294008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -20 \\\\ 20 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122867} {:answer/id #uuid "fecbf232-6df8-57f3-9219-aca9e2329182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122868} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9b4a40-3fe1-5d0d-9850-a2209f1ef66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122869} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2f7dc7-9e21-5e71-b60e-afd1c4183457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122870} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec212a5-da40-5312-9a85-4f50a1661f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec212a5-da40-5312-9a85-4f50a1661f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122871} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a623219-688e-5ead-a4e8-d6bdd81add41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b5330de-42e9-59fc-85b7-be3de824baa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37314" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $F$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbf75def-7838-52d3-b96b-fc0342a24a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306819} {:answer/id #uuid "a77e194c-6a54-53ce-b980-0f967fe9637a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -2048 & 1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306820} {:answer/id #uuid "10c8fb02-95ea-5efc-9ee1-ff86de37cafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306821} {:answer/id #uuid "18c69055-b581-5a1b-9659-252aec9a922a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306822} {:answer/id #uuid "678332ec-0eba-5caa-b803-684ace402a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c69055-b581-5a1b-9659-252aec9a922a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306822} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7050541-6ea5-5d57-be78-d4d4fda0b611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db23cb7f-7918-5f9f-a450-b8c78a653994" :question/math-academy-id "q-37358" :question/problem "Find ${G}^{10},$ where\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $G:$\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $G=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${G}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{G}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{10} & =P{D}^{10}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-2)^{10}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3458a6f-9c67-5e9e-bdae-7d70f0a69fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306826} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f2d51f-b847-5d55-b18a-a42942fcc046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306827} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea77758-35fa-5d1a-93fd-3e88e2d58ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306828} {:answer/id #uuid "29d4effd-0ff3-59ae-9e45-b14b22ffc070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306829} {:answer/id #uuid "75d71b7a-85b3-515f-8ff9-f8415953f187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3458a6f-9c67-5e9e-bdae-7d70f0a69fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306826} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2003b6f-0454-5988-8299-6a6315503fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1eed05-d07c-590e-b279-39088f439f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-37362" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $N$ given that \n\n$$\nN[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $N:$\n\n$$\nN={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $N$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d724b57-5449-5940-a718-c7c4cb2b3732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306833} {:answer/id #uuid "251bcc1e-ec1b-597d-a1ab-166e78dfcca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306834} {:answer/id #uuid "5eea1f06-512d-58bd-9701-80b684e18e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306835} {:answer/id #uuid "45a6c498-0337-5ab2-a1db-a52e45bd06b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306836} {:answer/id #uuid "9637dba8-bbcb-5c06-aa7a-a8937ffb55fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9637dba8-bbcb-5c06-aa7a-a8937ffb55fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306837} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c17535-17aa-57e0-b939-1ea12ada9105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be975a26-f633-5f30-9bfe-65c4d99c8d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37363" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $L$ given that \n\n$$\nL[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dc70a3-a710-5069-a3e9-0688a52c1e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306840} {:answer/id #uuid "465bf360-d9fd-5977-9449-6bdf5d4ebfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306841} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd6e85d-f4fc-549e-acfc-69f07c6a19c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306842} {:answer/id #uuid "41560e0d-2e1f-5429-8b60-a3c0fcfc96c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 48 \\\\ 12 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306843} {:answer/id #uuid "cb75ef29-4aff-5885-8af1-79121620c59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & -12 \\\\ 12 & -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cd6e85d-f4fc-549e-acfc-69f07c6a19c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306842} :answer-field/id #uuid "7285363a-7065-5a51-ae2e-7335e88d4dfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54219578-de33-5e25-844d-49a5a1e3f956" :question/math-academy-id "q-37601" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{16},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{16} & =P{D}^{16}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{16}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[8]{2})^{16} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[4]{2})^{16}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 48 \\\\ 4 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d2c70d0-5558-5dc3-91f3-50938b0af1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306847} {:answer/id #uuid "299bd6e2-a3bf-5ac8-9ff2-db442233cb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306848} {:answer/id #uuid "54e8f14a-3a88-53d9-81b6-a823513c8955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306849} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfe717e-f4c6-50ab-b810-ad2b79f27f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306850} {:answer/id #uuid "df575f48-2895-5f8d-b20a-3b0964b6e2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e8f14a-3a88-53d9-81b6-a823513c8955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306849} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84b9105-d873-55e8-adf4-ca7492432404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed6cad5f-0721-57d9-b366-6ace4eed9438" :question/math-academy-id "q-81210" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6d8c26-44b3-5e4f-ad6d-e6633a6be5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306854} {:answer/id #uuid "de0b2e39-08ed-5540-bf1c-48f2d1210f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -13 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306855} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f5e02-540f-5c3a-80f3-64c3ee565714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306856} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5ec694-89a2-55cf-abf7-6061dd86b269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306857} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb42baf-1488-57ad-92e7-f80031b92f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 4 \\\\ 15 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a53f5e02-540f-5c3a-80f3-64c3ee565714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306856} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f06b6d9-2515-534f-8422-8ee5a96cb474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "364858a4-93e6-583e-af87-93429d43e74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81215" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf1915ba-604d-5294-b5dc-d191cc4ef669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306861} {:answer/id #uuid "e9af0d92-c085-5d7f-9738-a45b8070d04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306862} {:answer/id #uuid "bc65cb7d-465a-5b73-8d9d-0d72827f1571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306863} {:answer/id #uuid "25787eae-9e41-5fde-9844-363aa4432844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306864} {:answer/id #uuid "41926a6a-ae20-5014-bfc5-ba6435b404c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41926a6a-ae20-5014-bfc5-ba6435b404c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306865} :answer-field/id #uuid "547e4226-1d32-5af9-b01b-7afd320c1134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef1b710-a3f0-5447-a9e9-c53014f598d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306868} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc45458-19b1-5438-9f0f-4ef79ff85392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306869} {:answer/id #uuid "232a5974-ded3-5a6c-a231-23c4eef5215b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306870} {:answer/id #uuid "8d839acd-7543-59a8-a16a-041acb3aaf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306871} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd04ec0-b98b-5749-ae26-f1005f26dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d839acd-7543-59a8-a16a-041acb3aaf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306871} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac6deca-d8f8-517a-a24a-93496cb68beb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f76ba1b-7ea5-5d91-91ae-a14e891b8be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-86676" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1b4959-c583-529a-9211-97adf3516fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306875} {:answer/id #uuid "b64d0d91-39ef-53eb-87da-9a4c32ae929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306876} {:answer/id #uuid "3eff7883-f932-5304-8e0f-f9232684000f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306877} {:answer/id #uuid "2dba43ca-55b6-5a37-9f55-4b93cfee110c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306878} {:answer/id #uuid "7af6bc2f-a185-5b7e-8bc5-db58066c2754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dba43ca-55b6-5a37-9f55-4b93cfee110c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306878} :answer-field/id #uuid "4879d6ea-de0e-5b4a-a1fa-d6809380822a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c35f9c27-fcab-5507-a7a4-2129f2a0b1bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86680" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b8945b-efb5-59d6-a22f-f8547284d667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306882} {:answer/id #uuid "78dc6699-9160-5797-98bc-848c8c7ecfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306883} {:answer/id #uuid "82eb7693-8038-584c-b089-a188c160e4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306884} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9d3324-8234-54fe-b58e-dbb51241f6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306885} {:answer/id #uuid "dfedba1e-eded-5b06-a787-2e70b484e347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b8945b-efb5-59d6-a22f-f8547284d667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306882} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c99d30-5ee5-56bf-9ce8-600b2a47f764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37463d01-e40c-576d-80b5-d846bd09bdbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-86682" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412bff98-3ac8-50cb-81e9-8c7013d07151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306889} {:answer/id #uuid "de9dfa26-ac2d-5aff-ba6a-a3c457318c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306890} {:answer/id #uuid "09c0069f-db49-5fc7-98f7-ebac210c797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306891} {:answer/id #uuid "9f50342e-c33f-5ed6-af50-69ae97a9b769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306892} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2821a2-5d15-5287-9113-bceca7ea6c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f50342e-c33f-5ed6-af50-69ae97a9b769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306892} :answer-field/id #uuid "07daf116-1bb1-55cf-9b1d-00d40c74951c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce43285b-7223-5171-8e80-52749669a7a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86691" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34dcf4-3d33-5cf6-99d0-8d38732150d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306896} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e75822-f6c7-57f3-99bb-2d9e03a5b79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306897} {:answer/id #uuid "9047b396-f04f-59d8-a461-eeb54ce85b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306898} {:answer/id #uuid "7f425780-7b2d-5b9b-9420-c7a5dc80e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306899} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc0935f-4830-501a-9899-68828e4eb6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9047b396-f04f-59d8-a461-eeb54ce85b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306898} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee2e222-e23f-5d55-af5f-ac55f758a6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa57c68c-4d50-562c-8052-052523362c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-86694" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"}]}
